use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Dismissal import Dismissal
from ../../../behaviors/Positioning import Positioning
from ../../../behaviors/Presence import Presence

export ui-component HoverCard:
    description:
        A hover card is a preview that pops up when the user points at a link,
        such as a person's profile card when pointing at their name. It shows
        what is behind the link without following it. It is a convenience;
        everything in it can also be reached by following the link.

    behaviors:
        - @{Positioning}
        - @{Dismissal}
        - @{Presence}

    showing:
        It appears when the pointer rests on the link for a moment, and also
        when the link gets keyboard focus. It waits longer than a Tooltip, so
        it doesn't pop up while the pointer is just passing over. The
        component can change how long it waits, and can turn the card off.

    hiding:
        It goes after a short pause when the pointer leaves both the link and
        the card, or when focus leaves the link. The pause lets the user move
        the pointer across onto the card. It also closes the ways Dismissal
        describes, such as Escape.

    content:
        Richer than a Tooltip: a picture, a few lines of text, and even links
        of its own. It may show a small arrow pointing at the link, which
        follows where Positioning actually put it.

    triggers:
        Several links can share one card, such as each name in a list. It
        attaches to whichever one the user is on, and moving between them moves
        it without closing it.

    assistiveTools:
        Assistive tools aren't told about the card, and pressing the link
        follows it as usual. Whether keyboard users can move into the card is
        up to the chosen library, which is why nothing may be available only
        in the card.

    nonGoals:
        - Not opened by pressing. A panel opened by pressing is a Popover
        - Not for plain-text hints. That's a Tooltip
